<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">copyover</ansi>

The <ansi fg="command">copyover</ansi> command live-restarts the server in place. The world
pauses for a few seconds, then resumes: telnet players keep their connection
straight through it ("Copyover complete."), web clients reconnect
automatically without seeing the login prompt, and the living economy
(shops, foraging, caravans) is flushed to disk first so nothing rewinds.

<ansi fg="yellow">Usage: </ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">copyover</ansi>

(Sending SIGUSR1 to the server process does the same thing. Unix only;
on Windows this command does nothing.)

<ansi fg="yellow">What it IS useful for:</ansi>

  - Restarting the world without kicking anyone: clearing stuck runtime
    state, misbehaving mob instances, or long-uptime cruft.
  - On a bare-metal install (binary run directly on the host), swapping in
    a freshly built binary or edited data files seamlessly — copyover
    re-executes whatever is on disk at the binary's path.

<ansi fg="yellow">What it is NOT useful for:</ansi>

  - Deploying new code on the production (Docker) host. The binary and all
    world data are baked into the container image — copyover re-executes
    that same baked binary forever. New code needs the full deploy
    (rebuild + container recreate), which is itself a cold restart; a
    copyover after it is redundant.
  - Updating what players' BROWSERS run. A web tab keeps the client script
    it loaded with the page; copyover deliberately keeps that page alive.
    Changes to the web client only reach a player when they reload the
    page, no matter how many copyovers happen.
  - Fixing anything stored in save files — those are re-read as-is.

If the restart fails mid-restore, the process exits and it behaves like a
normal crash/cold restart: players drop and the server must be started the
usual way. Nothing is lost — all state is saved to disk before the attempt.
